timestamp=2026-11-04T15:41:37.602Z level=INFO run=258c00cc message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\npatient_has_claim = {}\nfor r in rows:\n    pid = r['patient_id']\n    if r['record_date'].strip():\n        patient_has_claim[pid] = True\n# fill others\nfor r in rows:\n    pid = r['patient_id']\n    if pid not in patient_has_claim:\n        patient_has_claim[pid] = False\n\nec_of_count = defaultdict(int)\nfor r in rows:\n    ec = r['emergency_contact_patient_id']\n    if ec:\n        ec_of_count[ec] += 1\n\n# for each patient, get EC and see if EC has claims AND also see claim details of EC?\nno_claim_set = set(k for k,v in patient_has_claim.items() if not v)\n\n# also look at who files claims - the patient or someone else? if EC files for patient?\n# and claims where patient is the one? not sure. But claims exist with record_date. Who is the claimant?\n# look at the full rows - maybe the \"emergency contact\" relationship is key\n\n# check if any claim row has a patient with no_claim who is involved? maybe patient is the subject but someone else is their EC\n# also look at the EC field in claims - for patients WITH claims, do they also list EC?\nfor r in rows[:10]:\n    print(r)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
