timestamp=2026-11-04T15:51:31.538Z level=INFO run=258c00cc message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Now pick top 3. Clearly PAT_023 and PAT_174 are strongest (serious claims by EC).\n# Third? Could be PAT_028, PAT_105, PAT_190, PAT_273 - they all have EC with high-amount trauma and dependents with no claims.\n# Compare which is most plausible. Also look at structure - e.g. if patient has dependents who haven't filed, and the provider/guardian is gone?\n# PAT_273 has 3 deps, 1 of them has no claim (PAT_081). Also EC is PAT_217 who filed workplace fall injury with large amount.\n# Similarly for others - all have 1 dependent with no claim. Need to look more closely at the full context.\n# Also look at who is the claimant? The EC files the claim. If the patient is deceased, often the EC (next of kin) files serious claims.\n# But the question says \"patients that are most likely to be deceased\" - maybe also consider if the patient themselves is the one with trauma claim? No, these are patients with no claims.\n# Another hint: look at the dataset as a whole. Let us think differently - maybe \"compensation_reason\" like bereavement is paid out because someone died; the deceased person might be the one the claim is about? Or is the claimant the patient_id in the row?\n# If PAT_033 filed bereavement compensation (patient_id is PAT_033 in that row), that means PAT_033 is receiving/filing bereavement. That might imply someone related to PAT_033 died - possibly their spouse/child? Or maybe the deceased is the person listed as their EC in some contexts? Or look up common patterns.\n# But in our case, PAT_033's EC is PAT_011. And PAT_174 (no claims) has EC = PAT_033. That suggests PAT_174 is the one who died, and PAT_033 (next of kin/EC) filed bereavement. That fits perfectly.\n# Similarly, PAT_023 has no claims, EC is PAT_265 who filed family support adjustment. PAT_040 (a child/dependent with no claims) lists PAT_023 as EC. That fits - PAT_023 is likely the deceased parent, PAT_265 is the surviving spouse/guardian filing family support, and PAT_040 is a dependent.\n# So for the other cases: PAT_273 (no claims) has EC PAT_217 who filed large workplace fall injury claim. Dependents include PAT_081 who has no claims and lists PAT_273 as EC. That suggests PAT_273 might be the injured/deceased person, and PAT_217 is filing on their behalf? Or maybe PAT_273 is deceased due to workplace incident? That is plausible. Workplace fall injury is the reason for EC's claim - maybe the incident involved PAT_273?\n# That could also indicate fatality. That gives us a third strong candidate.\n# Compare PAT_273 vs others. All have dependents with no claims and ECs with large trauma claims. Let us look at the EC claims more - who are they? For example, PAT_217 filed workplace fall injury claim; maybe PAT_273 was the one who had the workplace fall? But PAT_273 has no claims themselves.\n# That would mean the injured party is PAT_273 but someone else (PAT_217) is the claimant/filing? Or maybe the patient_id in claim row is the claimant. That changes interpretation.\n# If patient_id in a claim row is the claimant (the one receiving compensation or filing), then the subject of the incident might be someone else. That fits better with emergency contacts.\n# So for bereavement compensation filed by PAT_033, the bereaved is PAT_033; the deceased is likely someone PAT_033 is connected to (like PAT_174, their dependent who lists PAT_033 as EC, and has no claims). That fits.\n# Also family support adjustment filed by PAT_265 - PAT_265 is supporting dependents (PAT_023's child PAT_040 lists PAT_023 as EC, but also who does PAT_265 support? Maybe PAT_040 or PAT_023's family). That also points to PAT_023 being deceased.\n# For workplace fall injury claim filed by PAT_217 with large payout - if PAT_217 is filing for someone who died in workplace fall, and that someone is PAT_273 (who has dependents listing PAT_273 as EC, and PAT_273 has no claims), that fits. That makes PAT_273 very likely deceased.\n# Similarly, burn injury by PAT_062 with large payout - if the burn victim was PAT_105 or PAT_194? PAT_105 has no claims, has dependent PAT_151 (no claims) who lists PAT_105 as EC. EC of PAT_105 is PAT_062. So if PAT_062 is the claimant for burn injury (large payout), maybe the burn victim was PAT_105 (the person PAT_062 is related to as EC)? That also fits - making PAT_105 a strong candidate for being deceased.\n# That gives us multiple candidates. Let us pick the top 3 by score.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
